A box in a vertical writing mode now exports its central baseline to a
parent it’s orthogonal to, unless its text is sideways. Blink’s
ComputedStyle::GetFontBaseline() picks the same: FontBaseline::kCentral
for vertical text that isn’t sideways, and kAlphabetic otherwise.

Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5 (the build
Playwright ships), all three give each row of vertical text in the test
(vertical-lr, vertical-rl, two lines, upright, and sideways vertical-rl)
the same offsets as Ladybird does now, and the imported WPT test called
align-items-baseline-column-horz.html passes all five of its subtests.
Only sideways vertical-lr text still differs: The engines put its
alphabetic baseline on the descent side of its line, and Ladybird on the
ascent side of that line.
